<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panduranga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panduranga was the southernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, around modern Phan Rang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-thiet">Phan Thiết</a> in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. After the Vietnamese conquest of Vijaya in 1471 it became the last centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> kingship, surviving as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> and later of the Nguyễn dynasty until its final annexation in 1832.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealth was concentrated in port-cities: Simhapura, a riverine port and political centre from about 400 CE, Amarendrapura and Visnupura in the north, Indrapura, Amaravati and Vijaya in the centre, Nha Trang, Virapura and <b>Panduranga</b> in the south, crowded with Chinese, Khmer, Malay, Viet, Arab and Indian traders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hinduism's last places in Southeast Asia are Trowulan, capital of Majapahit, the last great Hindu-Buddhist kingdom of Java, and <b>Panduranga</b>, the last Cham principality, annexed by Vietnam in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>They encouraged settlement south into former Cham and Khmer lands, absorbing <b>Panduranga</b> and the Mekong delta, and welcomed Chinese Ming loyalists who founded towns such as My Tho.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li></ul></section>
